#            99383b2 engine — the single-breakpoint OPM backsolve inverted a
#              model the allocation does not use. `_opm_allocate` splits
#              residual upside `fd / (fd + preferred)` where `fd` is common
#              *plus the option pool*; the backsolve split it on bare common,
#              because the pool was never passed to `opm_backsolve`. So the
#              equity value was calibrated against one cap table and then
#              allocated with another, and the round did not reprice to its own
#              PPS under the model that produced the opinion. On 8M common, a 2M
#              pool and 4M preferred behind a $10M preference at 65% vol over
#              four years, a $2.50 round solved to $18,014,475 — at which the
#              allocation values that preferred at $2.357. Solving the right
#              model gives $19,604,376 and moves the concluded FMV from $0.8587
#              to $0.9604: the old figure was 11.9% light, in the direction that
#              under-prices employee options, and the error grows with the pool.
#              The waterfall backsolve path was already consistent (it re-runs
#              the real waterfall, pool included) and `current_value.py` already
#              folds options in with a comment saying it matches the OPM
#              fallback — this path was the outlier. Missed until now because
#              the tests built their target PPS with the same bare-common
#              formula the solver used, so the two agreed with each other and
#              disagreed with the engine; the new ones assert the round trip
#              against `compute`'s own output.
#            020e074 security — `partners.logo_url` is a stored, caller-supplied
#              URL and rendering any report PDF for that partner fetched it
#              server-side, checked only for "parses" and "http(s)". Downloading
#              a deliverable was enough to make the estate GET
#              169.254.169.254, 127.0.0.1:3001 or anything on 10/8 from inside
#              its own perimeter. The PNG/JPEG sniff made it blind, not safe: a
#              blind GET still maps hosts and ports by how fast it fails. The
#              destination is now resolved and every address it returns must be
#              public — private, loopback, link-local, CGNAT, benchmarking,
#              multicast and reserved for both families, the v4 addresses inside
#              IPv4-mapped / 6to4 / NAT64, and `localhost` / `.internal` by
#              name. `redirect: 'follow'` had to go with it, since undici
#              resolves the chain itself and only the first URL would ever be
#              checked; hops are followed by hand, three at most, each
#              re-checked, under the one 3s deadline. The v6 check parses the
#              sixteen bytes rather than matching text, because `new URL()`
#              re-serialises `[::ffff:127.0.0.1]` as `::ffff:7f00:1`.
#            0ff9df8 report/analytics — the FMV trend chart and the analytics
#              series each carried their own copy of "every valuation of the
#              same client" and both scoped it to `user_id`. `firmClients`
#              groups the firm roster by `(partner_id, company_name)`, so a firm
#              whose engagements for one company were opened by two members had
#              one client on the console and two strangers everywhere history is
#              drawn — and the chart is suppressed below two points, so the
#              deliverable silently dropped the year-on-year comparison. Client
#              intake made this the normal case: a converted intake is owned by
#              whoever pressed Convert. One `sameCompanyFilter`, both callers;
#              direct clients keep owner scoping.
